  "hints": [
    "Searching the grid separately for each word repeats a lot of work when many words share a beginning.",
    "Put all words into a trie, then run one backtracking DFS from every cell that walks the grid and the trie together, stopping as soon as the letters so far are not a prefix of any word.",
    "Mark a cell as used before exploring its neighbours and unmark it afterwards. When you reach a node that ends a word, record it and clear the mark so it is reported once; delete trie branches that become empty to prune later searches."
  ],
  "solution": {
    "js": "function findWords(grid, words) {\n  const root = { next: new Map(), word: null }\n  for (const word of words) {\n    let node = root\n    for (const ch of word) {\n      if (!node.next.has(ch)) node.next.set(ch, { next: new Map(), word: null })\n      node = node.next.get(ch)\n    }\n    node.word = word\n  }\n  const rows = grid.length\n  const cols = rows ? grid[0].length : 0\n  const used = grid.map(row => new Array(row.length).fill(false))\n  const found = []\n  const steps = [[1, 0], [-1, 0], [0, 1], [0, -1]]\n  const dfs = (r, c, parent) => {\n    const ch = grid[r][c]\n    const node = parent.next.get(ch)\n    if (!node) return\n    if (node.word !== null) {\n      found.push(node.word)\n      node.word = null\n    }\n    used[r][c] = true\n    for (const [dr, dc] of steps) {\n      const nr = r + dr\n      const nc = c + dc\n      if (nr >= 0 && nr < rows && nc >= 0 && nc < cols && !used[nr][nc]) dfs(nr, nc, node)\n    }\n    used[r][c] = false\n    if (!node.next.size && node.word === null) parent.next.delete(ch)\n  }\n  for (let r = 0; r < rows; r++) {\n    for (let c = 0; c < cols; c++) dfs(r, c, root)\n  }\n  return found.sort()\n}\n",
    "py": "def find_words(grid, words):\n    root = {}\n    for word in words:\n        node = root\n        for ch in word:\n            node = node.setdefault(ch, {})\n        node[\"$\"] = word\n    rows, cols = len(grid), len(grid[0]) if grid else 0\n    used = [[False] * cols for _ in range(rows)]\n    found = []\n\n    def dfs(r, c, parent):\n        ch = grid[r][c]\n        node = parent.get(ch)\n        if node is None:\n            return\n        if \"$\" in node:\n            found.append(node.pop(\"$\"))\n        used[r][c] = True\n        for nr, nc in ((r + 1, c), (r - 1, c), (r, c + 1), (r, c - 1)):\n            if 0 <= nr < rows and 0 <= nc < cols and not used[nr][nc]:\n                dfs(nr, nc, node)\n        used[r][c] = False\n        if not node:\n            del parent[ch]\n\n    for r in range(rows):\n        for c in range(cols):\n            dfs(r, c, root)\n    return sorted(found)\n",
    "explanation": "Walking the trie alongside the grid means one DFS explores all words at once and abandons a path the moment it stops being a prefix of any word. Removing a word's end marker after reporting it avoids duplicates, and deleting emptied trie branches stops later searches from re-exploring words that were already found."
  },
